##### send 'H' at +15.8 s
Homing repeatability, 5 runs. Each run re-homes from the previous back-off.
One throwaway home runs first and is NOT counted: the first
home of a series starts from wherever the last command left
the mechanism, so including it measures approach history
rather than repeatability. Measured 2026-09-02: with the
first run counted, one series read 5 counts and another 2,
while the settled runs within each agreed to about 1.
Every run re-zeroes at its own switch point, so the
run-to-run scatter shows up as spread in the encoder reading
at the moment of the latch.

  throwaway home done; starting the counted series.

run 1:
  release ENC_LATCH 115 usteps, closing-to-release 640 usteps, 2518 ms
run 2:
  release ENC_LATCH 102 usteps, closing-to-release 639 usteps, 2242 ms
run 3:
  release ENC_LATCH 0 usteps, closing-to-release 625 usteps, 2227 ms
run 4:
  release ENC_LATCH 12 usteps, closing-to-release 628 usteps, 2227 ms
run 5:
  release ENC_LATCH 12 usteps, closing-to-release 629 usteps, 2226 ms

succeeded: 5 of 5
spread: 115 usteps (0.809 deg, 9.0 encoder counts)
  Record this in ../mouse-joystick-pcb/docs/bringup-log.md
  under Homing, WITH the homing velocity: the MAX6816's
  debounce delay scales the offset with speed, so a
  repeatability figure without a velocity means nothing.


